--- Finding (experimental_verification_of_the_nktg_law_using_nasa_neptune_data_2023_2024):
--- a list of text (or of truths) given a list whose items can fail is refused at the
--- FIRST assignment, with the two types the wrong way round.
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike:
---   …-1.co:16:1: cannot reassign 'xs' from array[result[str]] to array[str] — variable type cannot change
---     16 │ xs = ['a']
--- Expected: the refusal on line 18 (`xs = [str[int['1']]]`), the line that changes the type, worded
--- "from array[str] to array[result[str]]" — as it is for a list of numbers:
--- `xs = [1.5]` then `xs = [float['2']]` is refused at the second line, "from array to
--- array[result[float]]". The same happens with `[true]` then `[int['1'] gt 0]`
--- ("from array[result[bool]] to array[bool]" at the first line). In a long program the
--- message sent me to the wrong line: the first `cells = [...]` was a plain list of
--- text, the culprit a later `cells = [...]` inside a loop holding `fmt[...]` of a
--- value that can fail.
xs = ['a']
log[xs]
xs = [str[int['1']]]
log[xs]
